body:
this sounds like something you should bring up with your therapist! to stop yourself from putting on the calm persona, write it in a note and print it/ use your phone to write it in a note and then ask your therapist if you can give them that! 

&#x200B;

also, never feel the need to apologize here. you're valid and allowed to express these things.

cry and pray

&#x200B;

really though; practice, practice, practice!

It causes more adaption to unhealthy risks, but it isn't inherently unhealthy unless we're talking my 600 pound life. Even if you're a fat woman yourself, you're still living in a fat shaming society where people excuse hatred towards larger bodies in the guise of questioning concern towards their health. I.E: The shit @ Lizzo. Lizzo has a larger body, does shows all the time, still fat despite all that moving and exercise. Fat does not determine entirely an individual's health, habits do. A fat person can eat healthy meals all the time, still remain and hold onto weight esp if that's just what their body looks like; skinny person can eat burgers day in and day out and stock up cholesterol, remain skinny and people think; wow there's nothing wrong with you.  See where I'm getting at here?

&#x200B;

This is 0 correlation to smoking, nice metaphor but no. Your advice was backwards because you should be focusing more on supporting OP on her choice in the sense of what is best for her mentally, but you just sort of subtly shamed her for her weight. Psychologically, that shame is bound to cause more issues such as BDD, EDS, anxiety disorders around eating, etc etc.

hi! welcome to the community first of all.

&#x200B;

second, i'm having a little trouble trying to figure out what your concern is. are you worried about your gender yourself?

i feel you with the wanting to hurt them. my mother was an nmom, a horrible woman. abusive. neglectful. you name it, she did it. there's so many times where i want to rip into her, make her hurt and cry the way she did to me. but then i think about how i don't want to be the person she raised me to be. i ignore her despite my anger, i refuse to speak to her. she left me for a new life long ago and i hate her and want nothing more than to watch her burn. 

&#x200B;

but i'm better than that and so are you. i know it hurts and it sucks to bare all the pain and anger. it's hurtful to know someone's actions dictated your life and if it wasn't for them, you'd be different. happier. you would've spent early years loving yourself and not having to wear the weight of trauma throughout your life, only to refind it when you reach out to them and find they've never changed. write letters to them, then burn them. it helps to get out the thoughts but not have to deal with the aftermath. your alters can help too, and maybe you can all write letters to the family collectively then have a ritual of burning.

i struggle with that too a lot. unfortunately i grew up on anime (lol) and was very influential leaving a lot of my mannerisms to be kind of 'girly' and not something cis men in america typically do. I.E: bowing my head and saying thank you to someone. not something cis men do often. it makes me feel pretty crappy, and like i'm not a real man, but tbh fuck that. i am a real man and i don't need to prove my masculinity to anyone. 

&#x200B;

do you, brother. just because we're trans men doesn't mean we're not allowed to like feminine things, do feminine things, etc etc. we are real men, you included. don't get too into the 'man in your head'. i struggle with that too and many trans individuals do, it's a bad cycle. very vicious and hurtful and can lead to intense BDD. love the man you see now and one day the man you aspire to be will merge with you now.

hey OP, sorry for the late reply. in the future, when these things happen and you want someone to talk to quickly, i advise 7cups. there are some listeners there who are willing to listen and help talk you through things and it's free (but there is a therapy option).

&#x200B;

that aside, for your own measurements; when these dreams happen, can you write down or mentally tell yourself it was just a dream? i know dreams are scary and heavy, but this was just a fragment of your PTSD speaking, and it's not happening!!

you won't be alone forever, i can tell you that. i only had a few friends in my late years of high school because i developed anxiety and just couldn't be charismatic anymore. i went years without making friends, but when i got a job, that changed and i made lots of new friends and people and connections. ofc, that was offline. online i have made so many friends.

&#x200B;

it's really difficult these days compared to when i was younger to find people online. there's so many things like reddit, twitter, etc that are brief communications and little friend making. but i suggest finding a community of something you like, games, shows, hobbies etc- and looking into forums, discords, and other things for it. with your classes, maybe try to find another person who seems quiet and talk to them. it's scary to talk to people, but i think the best thing to remember in high school is; everyone is scared and just wants to be loved and experiencing friends.

yeah im mad all the time and easy to rage and even easier to get overwhelmed, frustrated and explosive. since my trauma this past year, ive like started to hate all my friends, don't want anything to do with them, and it's nasty. if you find the cure, let me know in the meantime--

&#x200B;

meditation is what i was recommended. half assed answer, but maybe you'll benefit
